{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015tWt7DSSV547D9gSEf41P5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis is a Go project that re-implements Unix utilities from specifications derived from C source code. The pipeline is: extract → specify → synthesize → verify. The project uses differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations built from docs alone diverge in observable behavior.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - One package per utility (main.go + tests)\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n\n\n- `pkg/sys/` - Platform-specific syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, colors, sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current State:** The `version` and `wc` utilities are implemented with tests, the testutils differential testing framework exists, and the sys package has started with fileinfo abstractions.\n\n**Releases:** Looking at the roadmap, rel00.0 covers foundational infrastructure that's mostly in place, rel01.0 has `wc` complete, and rel01.1 through rel01.3 cover `cat`, `sponge`, and other utilities that haven't been started yet.\n\nWait, I need to reconcile what the code_status says versus what's actually in the source code. The status shows everything as \"not started\" with zero test files, but the source code clearly has existing implementations for `version`, `wc`, and various utility packages. This means I should treat those packages as already existing and not propose reimplementing them.\n\nLooking at the releases in scope, I can see that rel00.0 has several use cases tied to packages that already exist in the codebase—format, sys, and testutils are all present. The magefiles use case is excluded by the constraint anyway.\n\nFor rel01.0, the wc command has a main.go file but appears to be missing differential tests. The remaining releases—rel01.1 through rel01.3—haven't had any code started yet for cat, sponge, and du respectively.\n\nFollowing the release-driven priority, rel00.0 looks mostly complete, so the next focus should be on rel01.0 to finish the wc implementation with proper test coverage. test.go)\n3. rel01.1: cmd/cat needs implementation\n4. rel01.2: cmd/sponge needs implementation\n5. rel01.3: cmd/du needs implementation\n\nThe constraint says I can only propose 1 task. Let me focus on the earliest incomplete work.\n\nFor cmd/wc, the main.go exists but there's no wc_test.go in the source_code. So the next task should be the differential tests for wc.\n\nWait, but I need to check -- is cmd/wc/main.go really complete? Let me look at the source_code. Yes, it's 356 lines and implements all the PRD requirements (R1-R6). It has flag parsing, counting, output formatting, etc.\n\nWhat's missing is the differential test file (wc_test.go). Following P5 (dependency ordering: test suites before code, but here code exists already), the next step would be tests.\n\nI'm looking at the requirement IDs embedded in the source code comments to understand what the test suite needs to cover. The wc implementation references prd005-wc with requirements R1 through R6, which break down into specific sub-requirements for input/output handling, counting operations, formatting and totals, binary input support, and error handling. So the tests should validate all these requirement areas. Looking at the test file naming patterns across the codebase, cmd/wc would follow the same convention with cmd/wc/wc_test.go. The differential test framework from pkg/testutils/difftest.go provides the structure I need—using DiffTest structs with fields for arguments, stdin, environment, working directory, exit codes, normalization, and expected files, then running them against both the Go implementation and the reference binary.\n\nFor the differential tests covering R1, R4, and R6, I need to set up the test file to build the Go binary, locate the GNU wc reference (gwc from Homebrew), and define test cases that exercise basic counting modes, various input sources including stdin and files, and error conditions like missing files and permission issues.\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the constraint more carefully—it says exactly 1 task, not multiple. Let me evaluate what's most impactful: the wc implementation exists but lacks tests entirely, so writing differential tests would complete rel01.0. But looking at rel00.0, I should verify whether the existing packages actually have test coverage—checking the source code, pkg/format has color_test.go, column_test.go, and size_test.go, pkg/sys has fileinfo_test.go and terminal_test.go, and pkg/testutils appears to have tests as well.\n\nSo rel00.0 seems complete, which means the wc differential test task is the right choice. Now I need to figure out what required_reading the stitch agent needs—the wc PRD, the test suite spec, the existing wc implementation, the testutils harness, and ideally an example differential test pattern to follow. But looking at the existing tests, I don't see a differential test example in the source code yet—the version_test.go uses something, but I need to find a proper pattern to reference.\n\nThe key insight is that the differential testing harness expects binary paths and runs them externally, so the test file needs to build the Go wc binary in TestMain and locate the reference binary before running the tests.\n\nI'm estimating this would be around 150-250 lines total, with each test case taking 5-8 lines and roughly 20-30 cases covering various flags and edge cases. The main requirements are building both binaries, testing the default counting modes with different input sources, and validating individual flag behaviors. flag combinations per prd005-wc R2, and R4 covers test output formatting with field width and --total modes plus error handling per prd005-wc R3 and R6.\n\nI'm thinking through the constraint that each task needs at most 4 requirements, so I'll structure this around the main test scenarios: setting up the test harness with a temporary binary and GNU reference, differential tests for individual flags and their interactions, and tests for stdin, file errors, and special modes.\n\nFor the design approach, I'm using the testutils.RunDiffTests framework where each test case is a DiffTest struct, relying on gwc as the reference binary with a skip if it's not available, and ensuring all tests run with LC_ALL=C per the architecture guidelines.\n\nThe acceptance criteria are straightforward: the test file compiles and runs, all differential tests pass when gwc is installed, coverage includes all counting modes and flag combinations, and tests gracefully skip when the reference binary isn't found.\n\nFor the scope, I'm creating just cmd/wc/wc_test.go since this is a test-only task—a single focused test file is more appropriate than spreading across multiple files.\n\nNow I need to decide on the test package convention. The project's version_test.go uses `package main` for direct function testing, but since I'm doing differential testing with compiled binaries, I should use `package main_test` to avoid needing internal access and keep the test isolated from the implementation.\n\nI should also check if there's an existing test suite specification I should reference—the code status shows no test infrastructure yet, but the spec should be complete in the docs directory, so I'll want to include that in my required reading.\n\nNow I'm examining the requirements in the implementation comments to understand what R1 through R6 cover, particularly to clarify what R5 involves since my task focuses on R1-R4 and R6. wc tests. I need to set up the test file with a TestMain function that builds the wc binary and finds the GNU reference implementation, then write differential test cases covering the default mode and individual flag combinations for both file and stdin inputs.\n\nNow I'm planning additional test cases for flag interactions like how -m overrides -c display, the various --total modes, and field width formatting across multiple files. I also need to handle error cases including missing files, the --files0-from option for reading NUL-delimited file lists, and ensuring binary input doesn't cause panics.\n\nFor the implementation, I'll use testutils.DiffTest structs with Name, Args, Stdin, and optional Env fields, running everything through the compiled binary rather than testing internal functions directly. The reference binary will be gwc from GNU coreutils, located via exec.LookPath, and if it's not available I'll skip the tests in TestMain instead of failing. All tests will use the default LC_ALL=C locale from testutils.buildEnv unless I'm specifically testing locale behavior.\n\nFor test data, I'll create temporary files in each test using t.TempDir with known content so the expected counts are deterministic, and set DiffTest.WorkDir for file-relative paths. The test suite needs to cover all six counting modes (default, -l, -w, -c, -m, -L) plus at least one flag combination, compile without errors, pass all differential tests when gwc is available, skip gracefully when it's not, and include at least one test that verifies error handling for missing files with the correct exit code and stderr output.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the constraint about having exactly one task. Looking at the cmd/wc/main.go structure and how differential tests work with testutils, I need to think through the package declaration—whether to use `package main` or `package main_test` for the test file, since the differential tests run compiled binaries and don't access unexported symbols.\n\nThe pattern from difftest_test.go shows how to set up TestMain to build the binary, find the reference implementation, and run the tests. For the wc test, I'd build the Go binary, locate the reference gwc command, and then execute the differential tests through RunDiffTests. Let me estimate the line count: package declaration and imports take about 15 lines, TestMain with binary building and cleanup around 30 lines, and then the test function with a slice of test cases covering various scenarios—each case roughly 5-7 lines, so 15-20 cases total should give a reasonable scope.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":51622,"cache_read_input_tokens":19292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":51622},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a7c1976-2bdb-43ee-8a56-6ee9167f63c6","uuid":"4364ccf0-8c52-4f9e-8b0a-87cfbd567b2a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015tWt7DSSV547D9gSEf41P5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (per-utility Go binaries), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, excluded from generation).\n\n**Implementation State (from source_code):**\n- **Complete:** `pkg/testutils/` (difftest.go + tests), `pkg/sys/` (fileinfo + terminal + tests), `pkg/format/` (color + column + size + tests), `cmd/version/` (main + tests)\n- **Partially complete:** `cmd/wc/` (main.go exists, no differential test file)\n- **Not started:** `cmd/cat/`, `cmd/sponge/`, `cmd/du/`\n\n**Current Release Priority:** rel00.0 infrastructure exists in source code. rel01.0 (wc) has implementation but no differential tests. rel01.1 (cat), rel01.2 (sponge), rel01.3 (du) are pending.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is **rel01.0** (wc). The implementation (`cmd/wc/main.go`, 356 lines) exists but the differential test file (`cmd/wc/wc_test.go`) does not. Per P5 (dependency ordering), tests must exist before moving to rel01.1. This test file is also the first utility to use `pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests`, establishing the pattern all subsequent utilities will follow.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Differential tests for cmd/wc (prd005-wc R1-R4, R6)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd005-wc.yaml (all wc requirements R1-R6)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-01.0.yaml (test case definitions for wc)\n      - cmd/wc/main.go (existing wc implementation to verify)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (RunDiffTests harness API, DiffTest struct, NormalizeFunc type)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest_test.go (example of building helper binaries and using TestMain)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/wc/wc_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests comparing Go wc binary against GNU gwc reference binary\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Implement TestMain that compiles cmd/wc to a temporary binary via\n          \"go build -o <tmppath> .\" and locates the GNU reference binary gwc\n          via exec.LookPath(\"gwc\"). If gwc is not found, print a skip message\n          and exit 0 so the test suite is skipped rather than failed. Clean up\n          the temporary binary after all tests complete.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Differential tests for default counting mode (lines, words, bytes\n          with no flags) and each individual counting flag (-l, -w, -c, -m, -L)\n          using both file input and stdin input, per prd005-wc R1 and R2. Include\n          at least one flag combination test (e.g., -lw) to verify multi-flag\n          output ordering per prd005-wc R2.6.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Differential tests for flag interaction where -m overrides -c display\n          per prd005-wc R2.3, all four --total modes (auto, always, only, never)\n          per prd005-wc R3.3, and field width formatting with multiple files per\n          prd005-wc R3.1.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Differential tests for error paths and special input: missing file\n          produces stderr message and exit code 1 per prd005-wc R6, --files0-from\n          reads NUL-delimited file list per prd005-wc R4.4, and binary input\n          (bytes with values > 127) does not panic per prd005-wc R4.2.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Use pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests as the sole test driver. Each test case\n          is a testutils.DiffTest struct with Name, Args, Stdin, and optional Env\n          fields. Do not test internal functions directly -- this file tests the\n          compiled binary only via the differential harness.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          The reference binary is gwc (GNU wc installed via Homebrew). Locate it\n          with exec.LookPath(\"gwc\"). If not found, print a skip message via\n          fmt.Println and call os.Exit(0) in TestMain so the entire test file is\n          skipped on machines without GNU coreutils installed.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          All tests inherit LC_ALL=C from the testutils.buildEnv default. Do not\n          set locale overrides. This matches ARCHITECTURE design decision DD6\n          (LC_ALL=C for current roadmap).\n      - id: D4\n        text: |\n          Create temporary test input files inside each test function using\n          t.TempDir(). Write known content to files so that expected counts are\n          deterministic. Pass the temp directory as DiffTest.WorkDir so both\n          binaries resolve relative file paths identically.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/wc/wc_test.go compiles without errors via \"go test -c ./cmd/wc/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: All differential tests pass when gwc is available (\"go test ./cmd/wc/ -v\" exits 0)\n      - id: AC3\n        text: |\n          Tests cover all six counting modes: default (lines+words+bytes), -l,\n          -w, -c, -m, -L, and at least one flag combination\n      - id: AC4\n        text: Test suite skips gracefully when gwc is not installed (exit 0, not exit 1)\n      - id: AC5\n        text: |\n          At least one test verifies error output for a nonexistent file path\n          (exit code 1 from both binaries, stderr contains the missing filename)\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":51622,"cache_read_input_tokens":19292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":51622},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a7c1976-2bdb-43ee-8a56-6ee9167f63c6","uuid":"ceb8d2a5-5735-453d-898d-bb0b967153fb"}
